Apache Tomcat 11 manual installation steps (Ubuntu/Debian)

NOTE: install-tomcat.sh in this repo does all of this automatically:
	sudo bash install-tomcat.sh

The steps below use 11.0.29. Check https://tomcat.apache.org/download-11.cgi
for the current release and change the version number if needed.
Tomcat 11 needs Java 17 or newer.


STEP1: Install Java.
	sudo apt update -y
	sudo apt install default-jdk -y
	java -version

	Note: if this shows a version below 17, run
	      sudo apt install openjdk-17-jdk-headless -y

STEP2: Download and install Apache Tomcat.
	STEP2_a: Download the archive.
		     wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/tomcat/tomcat-11/v11.0.29/bin/apache-tomcat-11.0.29.tar.gz
	STEP2_b: Create a dedicated user and the installation directory /opt/tomcat/
		     sudo useradd --system --home-dir /opt/tomcat --shell /usr/sbin/nologin tomcat
		     sudo mkdir -p /opt/tomcat
	STEP2_c: Extract the archive into the installation directory.
		     sudo tar xzf apache-tomcat-11.0.29.tar.gz -C /opt/tomcat --strip-components=1

STEP3: Configure administrator and manager user accounts.
	sudo vi /opt/tomcat/conf/tomcat-users.xml

	Add these lines just above the closing </tomcat-users> line.
	Replace ChangeThisPassword with your own password.

		<role rolename="manager-gui" />
		<role rolename="manager-script" />
		<role rolename="admin-gui" />
		<user username="manager" password="ChangeThisPassword" roles="manager-gui,manager-script" />
		<user username="admin" password="ChangeThisPassword" roles="manager-gui,admin-gui" />

STEP4: Enable remote access to the Manager and Host Manager apps.
	Edit both files:
		sudo vi /opt/tomcat/webapps/manager/META-INF/context.xml
		sudo vi /opt/tomcat/webapps/host-manager/META-INF/context.xml

	In each, comment out the RemoteAddrValve section as shown below.

		<!-- <Valve className="org.apache.catalina.valves.RemoteAddrValve"
		       allow="127\.\d+\.\d+\.\d+|::1|0:0:0:0:0:0:0:1" /> -->

STEP5: Set ownership and permissions of the installation directory.
	sudo chown -R tomcat:tomcat /opt/tomcat
	sudo chmod -R u+rwX,g+rX,o-rwx /opt/tomcat

STEP6: Create a systemd unit file so Tomcat runs as a service.
	Find your Java home first:
		dirname $(dirname $(readlink -f $(which java)))

	sudo vi /etc/systemd/system/tomcat.service

	Paste the following, replacing the JAVA_HOME value with the path printed above.

[Unit]
Description=Apache Tomcat
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=forking
User=tomcat
Group=tomcat
Environment="JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-21-openjdk-amd64"
Environment="JAVA_OPTS=-Djava.security.egd=file:///dev/urandom"
Environment="CATALINA_BASE=/opt/tomcat"
Environment="CATALINA_HOME=/opt/tomcat"
Environment="CATALINA_PID=/opt/tomcat/temp/tomcat.pid"
Environment="CATALINA_OPTS=-Xms512M -Xmx1024M -server -XX:+UseParallelGC"
PIDFile=/opt/tomcat/temp/tomcat.pid
ExecStart=/opt/tomcat/bin/startup.sh
ExecStop=/opt/tomcat/bin/shutdown.sh
SuccessExitStatus=143
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

STEP7: Reload systemd, enable the service on boot and start it.
	sudo systemctl daemon-reload
	sudo systemctl enable tomcat
	sudo systemctl start tomcat
	sudo systemctl status tomcat

STEP8: Open it in the browser.
	Allow inbound TCP 8080 in the instance's security group, then browse to:
		http://<instance-public-ip>:8080/
		http://<instance-public-ip>:8080/manager/html
